WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.838
Conditional branches use flag state to decide whether execution continues sequentially or jumps to another address.

00:00:06.838 --> 00:00:14.103
A comparison may set flags as if subtraction had occurred while leaving the compared accumulator value unchanged.

00:00:14.103 --> 00:00:17.095
Instruction-specific behaviour matters when predicting a branch.

00:00:18.095 --> 00:00:23.952
The comparison yields a zero difference, so Z is set and JZ transfers control to EQUAL.

00:00:23.952 --> 00:00:25.050
A remains 05H.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:28.710
The jump relies on the comparison’s flags still being current.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:31.673
Which flag is tested by JZ?

00:00:31.673 --> 00:00:34.290
JZ branches when the zero flag is set.
